## Summary

`src/common/pipes/validation.pipe.spec.ts > ValidationPipe > transform > should call plainToInstance for custom metatypes` intermittently fails on CI when tests run with v8 coverage. The mocked `plainToInstance` registers 0 calls in CI even though the SUT clearly calls it (other tests in the same file pass).

## Failure signature

```
AssertionError: expected "vi.fn()" to be called with arguments: [ [Function MyDto], { field: 'data' } ]

Number of calls: 0

❯ src/common/pipes/validation.pipe.spec.ts:100:31
   98|       await pipe.transform(value, { type: 'body', metatype: MyDto });
   99|
  100|       expect(plainToInstance).toHaveBeenCalledWith(MyDto, value);
     |                               ^
  101|     });
```

## Observed occurrences

- PR #6011 CI: https://github.com/alkem-io/server/actions/runs/25054522143/job/73390997714 (failed; passed on re-run)
- develop @ `42acdbdbf` CI: https://github.com/alkem-io/server/actions/runs/25048322310 (failed)
- Passes locally with and without coverage (`pnpm exec vitest run src/common/pipes/validation.pipe.spec.ts --coverage`)

## Root cause (suspected)

```typescript
// validation.pipe.spec.ts:13-15
vi.mock('class-transformer', () => ({
  plainToInstance: vi.fn().mockImplementation((_cls: any, value: any) => value),
}));
```

Under v8 coverage instrumentation the SUT's import of `class-transformer` and the test file's `import { plainToInstance } from 'class-transformer'` can resolve to different module instances. The factory creates a fresh `vi.fn()` per evaluation, so the spec's reference to `plainToInstance` is a different mock from the one the SUT calls — `toHaveBeenCalled` asserts on a mock that was never invoked.

## Suggested fix

Use `vi.hoisted` to keep a single mock identity across both imports:

```typescript
const mocks = vi.hoisted(() => ({
  plainToInstance: vi
    .fn()
    .mockImplementation((_cls: unknown, value: unknown) => value),
}));

vi.mock('class-transformer', () => ({
  plainToInstance: mocks.plainToInstance,
}));

// In tests:
expect(mocks.plainToInstance).toHaveBeenCalledWith(MyDto, value);
```

Alternative: replace the `vi.fn()` mock + `toHaveBeenCalledWith` assertion with a behavior assertion (e.g. spy on the SUT's output rather than the dependency), which side-steps mock identity entirely.

## Scope

Test-only fix. No production code changes.

## Summary

`test/schema-contract/perf/large-schema.spec.ts > Large schema diff performance > completes diff under 5000ms` fails intermittently because its mutation step uses `Math.random()` and can occasionally remove zero `field5:` lines, producing a diff with 0 entries that fails the `toBeGreaterThan(0)` sanity check.

## Failure signature

```
AssertionError: expected 0 to be greater than 0

❯ test/schema-contract/perf/large-schema.spec.ts:57:35
   55|     const elapsed = Date.now() - start;
   56|     // Basic sanity on volume
   57|     expect(report.entries.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);
     |                                   ^
```

## Root cause

```ts
function mutateSchema(base: string): string {
  return base
    .split('\n')
    .filter(line => {
      if (line.trim().startsWith('field5:') && Math.random() < 0.02) {
        return false; // simulate removal
      }
      return true;
    })
    .join('\n');
}
```

`260` types × 1 `field5:` line each × `p=0.02` per line. Expected 5.2 removals; **P(zero removals) = `0.98^260` ≈ 0.5%** — i.e. ~1 in 200 CI runs trips this test.

## Observed occurrences

- PR #6011 CI: https://github.com/alkem-io/server/actions/runs/25057857127/job/73403625470 (failed, this run)
- Prior runs on the same branch passed.

## Suggested fix

Replace the random mutation with a deterministic one, e.g. remove every Nth `field5:` line:

```diff
-      if (line.trim().startsWith('field5:') && Math.random() < 0.02) {
-        return false; // simulate removal
-      }
+      // Deterministic: remove every 50th field5 line so the test never
+      // produces zero diff entries while still exercising real volume.
+      const idx = field5Counter++;
+      if (line.trim().startsWith('field5:') && idx % 50 === 0) {
+        return false;
+      }
```

Or seed `Math.random` (e.g. via a small seedable PRNG) and assert a fixed expected count.

## Scope

Test-only fix.

